/*!
    \title Qt for HarmonyOS Native Window ID Example
    \example platform/harmonynativewindowid
    \examplecategory {Mobile}
    \meta tag {widgets,harmonyos,window}

    \brief Shows the platform IDs of the windows of a Qt application on
    HarmonyOS.

    Each window of the example shows the ID of its platform window, the
    \c window.Window that the window is shown in, and the ID of the display
    that the platform window is on. Platform APIs and other applications
    identify windows and displays by these IDs.

    A window gets its \c window.Window from
    QNativeInterface::QHarmonyWindow on the Qt thread, as a
    QHarmonyJsObjectRef handle. \c getWindowProperties() returns the IDs
    synchronously, so the example reads them with
    QNativeInterface::QHarmonyApplication::runOnJsThreadAndWait(), which
    returns the result of the task to the Qt thread:

    \snippet platform/harmonynativewindowid/main.cpp 0

    On the JS thread, the task dereferences the handle and reads the IDs
    from the window properties. The display ID is an optional property. An
    error that the call throws is returned as text:

    \snippet platform/harmonynativewindowid/main.cpp 1

    The constructor creates the window's QWindow and connects to
    QWindow::screenChanged(), so the IDs are read again whenever the window
    moves to another display:

    \snippet platform/harmonynativewindowid/main.cpp 2

    A window reads its IDs right after show() has returned, when its
    platform window exists:

    \snippet platform/harmonynativewindowid/main.cpp 3

    \section1 Running the Example

    Press \uicontrol {New sub window} and \uicontrol {New top-level window}.
    Each window shows a window ID of its own. A sub window is shown in a
    platform window of its own too.

    Move a window to another display. The display ID follows the window,
    while the window ID stays the same.
*/
